# See docs/adr/0012-ci-cd-ghcr-publishing.md for the full reasoning.
#
# Group 1: pip's own vendored copies of these libraries
# (pip/_vendor/msgpack, pip's internal setuptools bootstrap), not a
# package our code actually imports or depends on. Verified directly:
# `find /opt/venv -iname '*msgpack*'` in the built image shows only
# pip/_vendor/msgpack -- the package our code actually uses is a
# different, unrelated library (ormsgpack, unaffected).
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273

# Group 2: the *dormant* ensurepip bootstrap wheel that ships inside
# every python:3.12-slim image, not the actual installed pip (which our
# Dockerfiles upgrade via `pip install --upgrade pip` -- confirmed
# 26.2.1 in the built image). Verified directly: the file Trivy flags
# is /usr/local/lib/python3.12/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-25.0.1-py3-none-any.whl,
# a static file baked into the base image, never imported or executed
# (nothing in this project runs `python -m ensurepip`). The associated
# "setuptools" finding (no file path, `location: Python`) traces to the
# same bundled wheel's internal metadata, not an installed package --
# verified nothing named setuptools exists anywhere in the image
# (`find / -iname '*setuptools*'` returns empty).
# First seen 2026-08-12: these didn't appear in an earlier same-day
# local scan, then showed up in CI and a later local scan minutes
# apart with no image change in between -- Trivy's vulnerability DB
# updates continuously, so a scan of an unchanged image can start
# failing without any code change on our side. Worth knowing, not a
# bug: revisit whenever the base Python image bumps.
CVE-2026-1703
CVE-2026-8643
CVE-2026-6357
CVE-2026-3219
CVE-2025-8869
CVE-2026-59890

# Revisit all of the above when bumping the base Python image or pip version.
